window.DDIA = window.DDIA || {chapters:[], viz:[], system:null};
window.DDIA.chapters.push({
  n: 11, title: "Batch Processing",
  worksheet: "https://docs.google.com/document/d/12C76S4-MeUxf_eT3kAlWiA1IJxfeSebH0KAJjr9nvKs/edit",
  why: "Chapters 2 through 10 were about systems that answer a request while someone waits. This chapter is the other half of every real product: the jobs that run over everything you've stored, with nobody waiting, and produce something new from it: a search index, a model, a report, a backfill, a deletion proof. The interview trap is treating batch as a scheduling problem; the chapter's point is that it's a functional-programming problem. Immutable input, deterministic function, output you can throw away and rebuild. Every answer in this chapter comes back to that sentence. Two visualizations: the <a href=\"#/viz/batch-joins\">Batch Joins Stepper</a> and <a href=\"#/viz/etl-vs-elt\">ETL vs ELT</a>.",
  jargon: [
    ["Batch job", "Reads a fixed, complete input, runs for minutes to days, writes a complete output. Measured by throughput, not response time. Nobody waits on any one record."],
    ["Online / batch / stream", "Request-response, judged by response time; bounded input, judged by throughput; unbounded input, judged by the delay between an event and its effect. Three systems, three metrics."],
    ["Unix philosophy", "Small single-purpose programs, a uniform interface (a stream of lines), input never modified, logic separate from wiring. <code>sort | uniq -c</code> is MapReduce on one machine: sort is the shuffle, uniq -c is the reducer."],
    ["Immutable input, derived output", "The job only reads its input and writes to a new location. A bug means fix the code, discard the output, re-run. Nothing was destroyed. The property the whole chapter rests on."],
    ["Map", "One input record in, zero or more key-value pairs out. Filter, project, explode. Really flatMap; it can drop records, it just can't see two at once."],
    ["Shuffle", "Partition every emitted record to the reducer that owns its key (hash mod R), move it over the network, then sort by key so one key's values are adjacent. Where the network cost and the skew both live."],
    ["Reduce", "Walk one partition's sorted input once; all values for a key arrive together. Emits one output file per reducer; nothing merges across reducers afterward."],
    ["Combiner", "The reduce function applied on the mapper before the shuffle. Legal only for associative, commutative aggregations. Turns a hot key's million (user, 1) records into one per mapper."],
    ["Coordinator", "Hands out map and reduce tasks, watches completion, re-runs failures. Processes no data. A scheduler, not a chapter 10 leader: nothing for two of them to disagree about."],
    ["Data locality", "Input is cut into blocks (128 MB), each stored on three machines; the scheduler runs each mapper on a machine holding its block. The Hadoop-era picture; object storage weakened it."],
    ["Sort-merge join", "Both inputs through mappers emitting the join key, tagged by source; shuffle brings a key's records from both sides to one reducer; sort puts the small side's record first. Any size; everything crosses the network."],
    ["Broadcast hash join", "One side fits in memory. Copy it whole to every mapper, build a hash table, join as you read the big side. No shuffle. Breaks when the small side grows, usually silently over two years."],
    ["Partitioned hash join", "Both inputs already partitioned by the same key, hash and count. Mapper k loads only partition k of the small side. The broadcast join one partition at a time; the shuffle happened in an earlier job."],
    ["Hot key / skew", "One key with far more records than the rest lands on one reducer; the job finishes when that reducer does. No hash function can split one key. Fixes: combiner, salt plus replicated small side, map-side join for the hot keys, or a product cap."],
    ["Salt", "Append a suffix derived from the record (never a random draw) to a hot key so it spreads across N reducers; replicate the other side's record N ways for a join; sum N partials in a second stage for an aggregation."],
    ["Materialization", "Writing an intermediate result to durable replicated storage between stages. MapReduce did it after every job: restartable and debuggable, slow and no pipelining."],
    ["Dataflow engine", "Spark, Flink, Tez. The whole workflow is one graph of operators; output streams to the next operator in memory or local disk; recovery is recomputing a lost partition from its inputs. Same primitives, no forced sort, no do-nothing mappers."],
    ["Deterministic operator", "Same input, same output, every run. Required because a lost partition is recomputed after downstream tasks may already have read the first version; nondeterminism gives an output that silently mixes two versions."],
    ["Checkpoint", "Deliberately write one stage's output durably inside a dataflow job, to cap how far back a failure recomputes. MapReduce's behavior bought back for one stage."],
    ["Object storage + columnar files + table format", "Where batch data lives now: S3-style storage separate from compute, Parquet files (one column at a time, block statistics), and Iceberg/Delta/Hudi metadata giving the files a schema, snapshots, atomic commits and time travel. The lakehouse."],
    ["Warehouse vs lake", "A managed columnar system that owns its storage and enforces schema at load (Snowflake, BigQuery) vs open files you own with schema decided at read. The lakehouse blurs it. Not Postgres sharded."],
    ["ETL vs ELT", "Transform in code before loading, or load raw and transform with SQL in the warehouse. ELT keeps the raw copy (re-derive anything later); ETL moves less and masks before landing."],
    ["Bulk-loading the output", "A batch job writes the serving store's own files (index segments, sorted tables), the serving nodes copy them, a pointer flips atomically, the old set stays for rollback. Never one row at a time into a live database."],
    ["Derived data", "Anything regenerable from something else: an index, a cache, a view, a model, a feature table. The source is the truth; the derivation is a job; a wrong derivation is a code fix and a re-run."],
    ["Pregel / bulk synchronous parallel", "Think like a vertex: partition the graph once; in each superstep a vertex reads its messages, updates, sends to neighbors; a barrier between supersteps; stop when nobody sends. The graph never moves after partitioning; only messages do."],
    ["Stale vs incorrect", "Batch output is stale (behind, but right for its cutoff) and heals at the next run. An incrementally patched view is current and can be wrong forever: it has no memory of being correct. Only a rebuild from the source does."],
    ["Version by log position", "Every derived row carries the offset of the last event applied to it; a write lands only if its version is newer. Makes batch backfills and stream patches commutative. Last-writer-wins with a log position as the clock, which is the safe kind."]
  ],
  sections: [
    { id: "recap", title: "Recap questions", items: [
      { q: "Batch vs online vs stream: one sentence each, and the metric for each",
        robby: "Described batch and stream, skipped online entirely, gave no metrics. Also \"a con of batch is that it's huge and must be distributed\": distribution is batch's normal mode, and the framework does the merging.",
        a: "<p><b>Online:</b> a client sends a request and waits; metric is response time (p99). <b>Batch:</b> a bounded, complete input to a complete output, nobody waiting on any one record; metric is throughput, or how long the whole job takes. <b>Stream:</b> an unbounded input, output shortly after each event; metric is the delay between event and effect, plus lag when the consumer falls behind.</p><p>Batch's real pro, stated sharp: the input is immutable and the output derived, so a bug means fix the code and re-run. Nothing is destroyed.</p>",
        line: "three systems, three metrics: response time, throughput, event-to-effect delay." },
      { q: "Why does the chapter open with Unix pipes? What property of sort | uniq -c is MapReduce copying?",
        robby: "\"Unix tools churn through gigabytes without holding it all in memory; today it doesn't matter, use what you're comfortable with.\" The memory point is true; the chapter is making a design argument, not a speed one.",
        a: "<p>The pipe design is the thing the chapter generalizes: a uniform interface (lines; for MapReduce, key-value files), input never modified, logic separated from wiring so stages compose, re-order, and debug one at a time, and don't need to coordinate because they only talk through files.</p><p>The specific property: <b>sorting puts identical keys adjacent, so one sequential pass with one counter finishes each key.</b> That is the shuffle. <code>sort</code> is the shuffle and <code>uniq -c</code> is the reducer.</p>",
        line: "sort is the shuffle, uniq -c is the reducer; the lesson is composable stages over immutable input, not shell vs Python." },
      { q: "Walk one MapReduce job end to end (count messages per user). Where is the network cost?",
        robby: "\"The map is from user ID to count.\" No: the mapper emits (user, 1), the reducer counts. Also \"split the file and merge chunks without crawling\": merging per-chunk counts <i>is</i> the shuffle. And \"hash by user to save network trips\": the hash decides destination, it doesn't reduce traffic.",
        a: "<p><b>Input:</b> the log in 128 MB blocks, each on three machines, ordered by time. <b>Map:</b> runs where the block is (no network), emits <code>(user_id, 1)</code> per record. <b>Shuffle:</b> partition each record to reducer <code>hash(user_id) mod R</code>, then sort within the reducer. <b>Reduce:</b> one pass, sum per user, one output file per reducer.</p><p><b>The network cost is the shuffle:</b> one record per message crosses. A <b>combiner</b> pre-sums on each mapper so Alice arrives as one record per mapper instead of one per message. Output replication is a second, smaller cost. The merging across mappers is done by the R reducers independently, each pulling its hash range from every mapper; nothing merges across reducers afterward.</p>",
        line: "mappers read locally and emit pairs, the shuffle partitions and sorts, reducers aggregate once; the cost is the shuffle and a combiner shrinks it." },
      { q: "Who's in charge, and who does the merging?",
        robby: "\"Would every node have all the data? Who's the leader that merges once the mapping is done?\" Replication is per block, not per file; nobody merges singly.",
        a: "<p>A 1 TB log is ~8,000 blocks, each stored on three of the thousand nodes; each node holds ~3/1000 of the data. A <b>coordinator</b> hands out tasks and retries failures; it processes no data and needs no consensus because it holds nothing two coordinators could disagree about. Each of R reducers pulls its slice from every mapper, merges the already-sorted pieces, and reduces. R reducers in parallel, no coordination between them. The output is R files; that is the answer.</p>",
        line: "placement is per block, the coordinator is a scheduler not a leader, and each reducer merges only its own slice." },
      { q: "Sort-merge join vs broadcast hash join: when each, and what breaks each",
        robby: "\"Sort each block, then merge by user ID; user IDs hash across multiple blocks so you sub-partition and publish the change.\" Nothing sorts input blocks; the shuffle sorts after partitioning by key, and input placement is unrelated to key placement.",
        a: "<p><b>Sort-merge:</b> mappers read both datasets and emit the join key tagged by source; shuffle brings a user's U record and all their M records to one reducer; sort puts U first; reducer holds the country and emits a joined row per message. Any size; both datasets cross the network; a hot key stalls one reducer.</p><p><b>Broadcast hash:</b> the users table fits in memory, so copy it to every mapper, build a hash table, join as you read messages. No shuffle. Breaks when the small side stops fitting, or when users × mappers becomes the cost.</p><p>Rule: both big, sort-merge. One side fits in RAM, broadcast.</p>",
        line: "both sides big → sort-merge and pay the shuffle; one side fits in memory → broadcast and pay users × mappers." },
      { q: "A hot key: what it does to runtime, and two ways to handle it",
        robby: "\"Depending on the hashing algorithm this could put extra load on one node.\" No hash can split one key. \"Use a more powerful machine for hot keys.\" Doesn't scale and no framework does it.",
        a: "<p>All of Taylor's records land on one reducer; a thousand-machine job runs at the speed of one. <b>Fixes:</b> salt the key with a suffix derived from the record (two stages for an aggregation; replicate the other side for a join); broadcast the hot keys' small-side records and map-side join them, sort-merge the rest; a combiner, which alone removes the skew for a plain count. Finding the hot keys: a sampling pass or table metadata.</p>",
        line: "no hash splits one key; salt it, map-side join it, or pre-aggregate it, and find it first with a sampling pass." },
      { q: "Does a map-side join for hot keys still pile the hot key on one machine?",
        robby: "\"Or is it spread out because the data is randomized pre-mapping?\"",
        a: "<p>Spread out, and not because of randomization: <b>mappers are assigned by input block, reducers by key.</b> Taylor's messages are scattered through the log by time, so each mapper sees only the ones in its block and joins them right there. Skew only appears when you group by key, and a map-side join never groups. A mapper's workload is capped by block size; a reducer's is capped by nothing. What gets broadcast is tiny: the other side's records for the hot keys only.</p>",
        line: "mapper work is bounded by the block, reducer work by nothing; skew is born at the shuffle." },
      { q: "Why did dataflow engines replace MapReduce, and what did they keep?",
        robby: "\"The whole workflow is one job, so they can work ahead.\" Right headline; the specifics and the second half were missing.",
        a: "<p><b>MapReduce wasted:</b> every stage's output written to the distributed filesystem three ways to be read once; no stage starts until the previous finishes entirely; a forced sort on every shuffle; mappers that only re-read the previous reducer's output. A whole-graph view also lets an optimizer reorder joins and push filters down.</p><p><b>Kept:</b> the primitives (partition by key, shuffle, sort-merge and hash joins, group and aggregate), the functional contract (immutable input, no side effects), fault tolerance by re-execution, and the storage layer for inputs and final outputs. Recovery changed shape: re-read a materialized file became recompute from inputs.</p>",
        line: "same primitives and the same contract; dropped the forced disk writes, the forced sort, and the forced waiting." },
      { q: "Why must a dataflow operator be deterministic, and what is the failure when it isn't?",
        robby: "\"If you change the order the operators run in, you get different answers on recompute, which is bad.\" Right conclusion, wrong mechanism, and it undersells the failure.",
        a: "<p>A node dies <i>after</i> some downstream tasks already consumed its output. The engine recomputes the lost partition; if the operator is nondeterministic, the recomputed partition differs, and the tasks that read it after the failure see a different version from the ones that read it before. The final output <b>mixes two versions of the same data</b>: records counted twice, records missing, join rows that match nothing, and the job reports success.</p><p>Concrete: salt a hot key with a random number, re-run a mapper after a failure, and some of Taylor's messages go to two reducers and others to none. Derive the salt from the record instead. Usual sources: random without a seed, the clock, hash-table iteration order, an external lookup that changed. Remedies: make it deterministic, recompute everything downstream too, or checkpoint that stage.</p>",
        line: "nondeterminism plus recompute-on-failure gives an output that silently mixes two versions; the job still says success." },
      { q: "Why never write batch output row by row into a live database? What instead?",
        robby: "\"Load on production, locks; write it somewhere offline and keep them separated.\" One of three reasons, and \"separated\" doesn't say how the output reaches users.",
        a: "<p><b>Three reasons:</b> load (a thousand tasks at batch speed swamp a database sized for users); throughput (one round trip per record is orders of magnitude slower than a sequential file); and <b>it breaks all-or-nothing</b>: a failed task's rows are already visible, the retry writes them again, and frameworks run duplicate copies of slow tasks on purpose.</p><p><b>Instead:</b> the job writes the serving store's own files (index segments, sorted immutable tables) to the distributed filesystem under the same all-or-nothing guarantee; serving nodes copy them while still answering from the old set; a pointer flips atomically; the old set stays for rollback. Softer alternative: write to a log and let the store consume at its own rate with idempotent writes (chapter 12).</p>",
        line: "build the store's files inside the job, copy, flip a pointer, keep the old set; never one row at a time." },
      { q: "Immutable input, throwaway output: what does that buy you when the job has a bug?",
        a: "<p>Re-run as many times as it takes; the bug cost compute and nothing else. Because tasks have no side effects, the framework can also re-run failed tasks automatically; the same property covers code bugs and machine failures. <b>Rollback:</b> the previous output is still there because the new run wrote to a new location, so serve yesterday's while you fix it, unlike a database that received bad writes. <b>Speed of change:</b> a mistake is cheap and reversible, so risky changes ship. The condition: no side effects outside the output files.</p>",
        line: "re-run, roll back, and move fast, all from one rule: read-only input, output to a new place, no side effects." },
      { q: "What changed in the 2nd edition's version of this chapter?",
        robby: "\"Object storage, giant blobs, driven by multimodal models and things like Snowflake.\" Object storage is right; the files are highly structured, and the driver was cost and elasticity, not models.",
        a: "<p><b>Where:</b> object storage with storage separated from compute; a cluster is rented for the job and discarded, and data locality matters less. <b>Format:</b> columnar files (Parquet), one column at a time, compressed, block statistics for skipping. <b>On top:</b> table formats (Iceberg, Delta, Hudi) that give a pile of files a schema, snapshots, atomic commits and time travel; the atomic commit is the file swap generalized. <b>Interface:</b> SQL and dataframes with an optimizer, nobody hand-writes map and reduce, and warehouses and batch engines converged on the same storage.</p><p>What's loose in a lake is <i>when</i> the schema is enforced (read, not load), not whether the data has shape.</p>",
        line: "object storage, Parquet, a table format on top, SQL in front; schema-on-read, not shapeless blobs." }
    ]},
    { id: "tradeoffs", title: "Mechanisms as tradeoffs", items: [
      { q: "A batch job (vs doing the work online at request time)",
        robby: "\"Use it when you don't care about recency; breaks when you need recency.\" True, but answered against stream, not online, and the \"when\" was one case.",
        when: "The work is too expensive to do while someone waits (a scan or join over everything); the answer can be computed ahead for every key and served as a lookup (recommendations, a search index, per-user aggregates); you want the result re-runnable. Precompute in batch, serve online.",
        breaks: ["Staleness: as old as the last run.", "Cold start: a new user has no precomputed row until the next run; you need an online fallback.", "Wasted work: results for every key, most never asked for today.", "The window: a job longer than its period never catches up.", "Late failure: a job that dies in hour eight cost eight hours."],
        line: "precompute in batch and serve by key; pay staleness, cold start, and the window." },
      { q: "MapReduce: two callbacks, output materialized between stages",
        robby: "Conflated intermediate files with materialized views, and said materialization \"breaks when nondeterministic.\" Backwards: materialization is the <i>cure</i> for nondeterminism, since every retry reads the same written file.",
        when: "Stages fail often (the original setting: tasks routinely killed for higher-priority work); you're debugging or building one stage; several downstream jobs read the same intermediate dataset, so it isn't really intermediate.",
        breaks: ["Every intermediate dataset written and replicated three ways to be read once.", "No pipelining: a stage waits for the previous one to finish entirely.", "The two-callback straitjacket: a join plus a group plus a sort is three jobs with do-nothing mappers, hence SQL layers on top.", "A forced sort on every shuffle."],
        line: "materialize when restart or reuse earns it; the intermediate file is scratch, not a view." },
      { q: "A dataflow engine: pipelining between operators, recompute on failure",
        robby: "\"Pipelining in memory: all the analysis in memory, or the DAG in memory with outputs on disk?\" Neither: what's skipped is the durable replicated write, not disks; shuffles spill to local disk.",
        when: "The default for new batch work. Fused operators pass records as function calls; shuffle boundaries buffer in memory and spill to local, unreplicated disk. Strongest for iterative work (graphs, model training) where the same dataset is reused every round.",
        breaks: ["Long chains: a late failure recomputes most of the job; add a checkpoint at expensive or widely shared stages.", "Nondeterministic operators make recomputation unsafe.", "Skew and memory: the shuffle is still there; a hot key spills and crawls instead of failing cleanly."],
        line: "dataflow by default, checkpoints at expensive stages, deterministic operators so recompute is safe." },
      { q: "Sort-merge join",
        when: "Neither side fits in memory, or you don't know the sizes. Works at any size, so it's the safe default.",
        breaks: ["Both datasets cross the network: the most expensive join in traffic.", "Hot keys stall one reducer.", "You pay to sort both sides even if you only wanted to match them.", "Wasted when the inputs are already co-partitioned (see partitioned hash join)."],
        line: "the join that always works, paid for with a shuffle of both sides and exposure to skew." },
      { q: "Broadcast hash join",
        when: "One side fits in memory on every mapper. No shuffle for the big side; a mapper's work is capped by its block, so no skew.",
        breaks: ["The small side stops fitting, silently, after two years of growth: the job that ran yesterday dies of out-of-memory today with no code change. Needs a size check and a fallback.", "Copy cost is users × mappers, so a big cluster makes a medium table expensive.", "Hash table build time on every mapper before the first record.", "Optimizer thresholds flip plans night to night when the table hovers near the limit."],
        line: "ship the small side everywhere; guard it with a size check, because the small side grows." },
      { q: "Partitioned hash join",
        robby: "\"I forget what this is.\" Then: \"Could the setup be a mapper stage on top of broadcast join?\" The setup is a whole job (a shuffle), which is exactly why it only pays when already done.",
        when: "Both sides large and already partitioned the same way: same key, same hash, same count. Mapper k loads only partition k of the small side; three independent small joins, no shuffle. Pays when an earlier job wrote both inputs that way, or when big tables are bucketed once at ingest and joined many times.",
        breaks: ["The partitioning must be identical; independently produced datasets almost never match and two different systems never do.", "The engine must know the layout from table metadata.", "Serves one key: bucketed by user helps no join on conversation.", "Done just for one join it saves nothing: producing co-partitioned inputs is a shuffle.", "Skew inside a partition still hurts."],
        line: "the broadcast join one partition at a time, on inputs some earlier reducer stage laid out; chapter 7's co-located shards." },
      { q: "Splitting a hot key across reducers",
        robby: "\"Costs you more nodes.\" No: it spreads over reducers you already have. And \"user ID + month\" salts an aggregation but doesn't fix a join without replicating the user record twelve ways, and one hot day is still one reducer.",
        when: "One reducer would run far longer than the rest and the job waits for it. Salt with a suffix derived from the record; replicate the other side N ways for a join; sum N partials in a second stage for an aggregation; nothing extra for a join's output.",
        breaks: ["Needs a hot-key list first: a sampling pass or table metadata, and it goes stale.", "The salt must be deterministic or a retried mapper double-counts and drops records.", "N copies of the small-side record per hot key.", "A second stage for aggregations.", "Two code paths if you go hybrid (map-side for hot, sort-merge for the rest)."],
        line: "salt from the record, replicate the other side, and know which keys are hot before you start." },
      { q: "Object storage + columnar files + a table format (the lakehouse) instead of a warehouse",
        robby: "Answered chapter 4's columnar question: \"use it when you need certain columns; breaks when you need every column.\" True of Parquet alone; the mechanism is the stack.",
        when: "Many engines need the same data (Spark for the index, a model trainer, a deletion job, SQL for analysts) and one copy in open files serves them all; store raw first and decide the schema at read; storage and compute scale and bill separately; cost at petabyte scale dominates.",
        breaks: ["Small files: per-request pricing and slow listing; a stream writer dropping a file a minute makes millions, and compaction is now your job.", "No in-place update: an object is replaced whole; the table format fakes updates with new files plus a commit and deletes with tombstone files.", "No point lookups: fetching one row decompresses a block per column; an analytics store, never a serving store.", "Concurrent writers need a catalog that serializes commits.", "The swamp: nothing forces schema or ownership; warehouses sell governance for a reason.", "Reading every column is no better than row storage; writing one row at a time is far worse."],
        line: "lakehouse when many engines need the data and cost dominates; warehouse when you want someone else to run maintenance and governance; most end up with both." },
      { q: "ELT (load raw, transform in SQL) vs ETL",
        robby: "\"Use ELT when you need atomicity, because SQL guarantees it.\" Atomicity isn't what separates them; a file-writing job has the same all-or-nothing property. The 99%-discard point was right.",
        when: "You want to keep the raw data (re-derive anything later when the transform was wrong or next year's question needs a dropped field); storage is cheap so loading everything is cheap; transforms as SQL are versioned, tested, and owned by analysts; many consumers derive different views from one raw copy. See the <a href=\"#/viz/etl-vs-elt\">ETL vs ELT</a> page.",
        breaks: ["Sensitive data lands raw; compliance often forces masking before the load, which is ETL by definition.", "Transforms that aren't SQL (parsing binary formats, running a model, calling a service) need a code step anyway.", "Compute cost moves to the metered warehouse; a popular model re-run hourly becomes the biggest line on the bill.", "Model sprawl: hundreds of SQL models and nobody knows which are used.", "A huge source where you need a sliver: load the sliver."],
        line: "ELT keeps the raw copy and makes the transform SQL; ETL moves less and masks before landing." },
      { q: "Building the output as immutable files and swapping them in atomically",
        robby: "\"Overwriting immutable files, swapping atomically, so they're not really immutable? Breaks when you run out of memory?\" The files are immutable; the <i>pointer</i> changes. And it's disk, not memory.",
        when: "Any batch output that a serving system reads: the job writes a new version to a new location, serving nodes copy it, a pointer (symlink, manifest, catalog row) flips, the old version stays. Same trick as table-format commits and LSM segments.",
        breaks: ["Disk: two full copies coexist at the swap; old versions pile up until pruned.", "The flip is atomic, the warm-up isn't: cold caches spike latency for minutes; pre-warm or shift traffic gradually.", "A one-record change means a full rebuild, which pushes you toward incremental (chapter 12).", "The pointer itself needs care: two publishers racing on a shared file can point at a half-copied directory; put it in a coordination service or a catalog with compare-and-set.", "Staleness, as always."],
        line: "new version plus pointer flip; nothing is overwritten, and rollback is pointing back." },
      { q: "Rebuilding derived data from scratch vs patching it incrementally",
        robby: "\"Rebuild if nondeterministic; patch incrementally I just wouldn't; no rollback plan.\" Nondeterminism isn't the deciding factor, every stream-maintained view is a patch, and both have rollback.",
        when: "<b>Rebuild</b> when the full job fits the window or correctness matters more than freshness: correct by construction, nothing drifts, a bug fix is a re-run. <b>Patch</b> when the change rate is tiny relative to the total and the output must be fresh: the inbox copy, an unread counter, a cache. In practice both: patch for freshness, rebuild on a schedule to reconcile so drift has a bounded lifetime.",
        breaks: ["Rebuild: cost and time scale with the whole dataset, not with what changed, and the output is a day stale.", "Patch: drift. A mis-handled event leaves a wrong state forever; deletes and out-of-order updates are hard; the state's correctness depends on every version of code that ever touched it.", "Rollback differs: rebuild keeps the previous output and re-points; patch keeps the input log and a checkpoint and replays with fixed code."],
        line: "a patched view has no memory of being correct; patch for now, rebuild for right, diff to find out how far apart." },
      { q: "Graph processing (Pregel) vs a join per hop",
        robby: "\"I don't know what the Pregel model is.\" Back-explained correctly afterward, minus the word <i>barrier</i>.",
        when: "Iteration count is large or unknown (PageRank, shortest paths, connected components) and the graph needs a cluster. Partition vertices once; supersteps separated by a barrier: read messages, update, send to neighbors; stop when nobody sends. The graph never moves; only messages do; late rounds touch only vertices still changing; fault tolerance by periodic checkpoint of all vertex state. A join per hop reshuffles the entire edge table every iteration.",
        breaks: ["The graph must fit in cluster memory.", "Partitioning decides everything and real graphs partition badly: a social graph has no clean cut, so most messages cross machines anyway.", "The barrier waits for the slowest machine; a celebrity vertex with fifty million edges sets the superstep's time.", "Small graphs: a single-machine algorithm wins."],
        line: "one or two hops, join; many rounds on a big graph, Pregel, and the barrier makes skew the cost again." },
      { q: "Reading from the production database vs a snapshot vs its change log",
        robby: "\"Direct read when you need fresh data and load is low.\" A two-hour scan of a live database isn't fresh, it's smeared: rows from minute 5 and minute 115 come from different states. And the change-log option went unanswered.",
        when: "<b>Direct:</b> a small table on a quiet night, and nothing else. <b>Snapshot:</b> a consistent point in time, no production load, re-runnable, a schema contract; the simple reliable default. <b>Change log:</b> stream the database's replication log into the lake continuously: consistent order, near-zero staleness, replayable history, many consumers, no load on the primary beyond one more replica.",
        breaks: ["Direct: load on users, no cutoff (a transaction counted in both states or neither), coupling to the live schema; a read replica fixes load but still smears.", "Snapshot: stale by the interval; a full copy of a big database every night.", "Change log: a pipeline to operate instead of a nightly copy, and reconstructing \"the table as of midnight\" from a stream takes real machinery (chapter 12)."],
        line: "direct only for a small table on a quiet night; snapshot for simple and consistent; change log when you need fresh or many readers." }
    ]},
    { id: "mock", title: "Batch in the messaging system", items: [
      { q: "Six batch jobs the system needs: input, output, cadence, who reads it",
        robby: "Found one (a hot-key sampler) and then \"most of these are going to be analytics; the inbox shouldn't be a batch job, it's cached.\"",
        a: "<p>The frame: a batch job's output is anything the product <i>serves</i> that's too expensive to compute per request, plus anything that must be proven to have run. Analytics is the smallest category.</p><table><tr><th>Job</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th><th>Cadence</th><th>Reader</th></tr><tr><td>Search index rebuild</td><td>messages snapshot, participants</td><td>(user, term) segments, swapped in</td><td>nightly</td><td>search service</td></tr><tr><td>Inbox copy backfill + reconcile</td><td>messages, participants</td><td>per-user inbox rows, diff vs live</td><td>weekly, and on schema change</td><td>inbox read path; on-call if diff ≠ 0</td></tr><tr><td>Deletion + retention</td><td>deletion requests, all stores</td><td>tombstones per store, signed report</td><td>daily</td><td>regulator; compaction</td></tr><tr><td>Abuse model + risk scores</td><td>messages, reports, blocks, account age</td><td>model file, per-user score table</td><td>daily</td><td>send path classifier, trust & safety</td></tr><tr><td>Product metrics</td><td>event log</td><td>DAU, msgs/day, delivery p99, group-size distribution</td><td>hourly ops, daily product</td><td>dashboards, on-call, leadership</td></tr><tr><td>Cold tiering</td><td>messages older than a year</td><td>files in object storage + pointers</td><td>monthly</td><td>old-history reads; the storage bill</td></tr></table><p>Plus the hot-key sampler (output: the hot list only), a per-user data export, people-you-may-know, storage quotas. The inbox correction: the live inbox is stream-maintained, and the <i>backfill</i> and <i>reconcile</i> are batch. Online serves, stream patches, batch rebuilds and checks.</p>",
        line: "batch output is what the product serves but can't compute per request; the inbox's backfill and reconcile are batch even though its freshness isn't." },
      { q: "Why does a stream-maintained view drift? Isn't batch the one that gets stale?",
        robby: "\"A stream seems like it would stay current while a batch would drift because it gets stale.\"",
        a: "<p><b>Batch goes stale. Stream goes incorrect.</b> Staleness heals at the next run; incorrectness never does. The inbox copy is never computed, it's the sum of every delta ever applied, and each of these leaves a permanent dent: a duplicated event (at-least-once delivery, crash between write and offset commit, unread count off by one forever); a dropped event; out-of-order (a delete arriving before the send it refers to); old code (a bug from March to June fixed in June leaves every row it touched wrong); a schema change (rows from months one through six have nothing in the new column). Lag reads zero the whole time.</p><p>The batch rebuild ignores the stream and recomputes from the source of truth, correct by construction for its cutoff. Diff it against the live copy as of the same cutoff; every mismatch is a drift bug. If the diff is always zero you've earned trust and can rebuild less often.</p>",
        line: "an incrementally maintained view has no memory of being correct; only a rebuild from the source does." },
      { q: "Stitching a weekly batch to the live stream: a new field added Monday, the batch ran Tuesday midnight, a message arrived at 12:01. How do you avoid clobbering?",
        robby: "Robby's own question, and the best moment of the session.",
        a: "<p><b>The cutoff is a position in the log, not a time on the clock.</b> The batch records that it read everything up to offset X; the 12:01 message is X+1.</p><p><b>Option A, version every row by log position.</b> Batch rows carry <code>version = X</code>; the stream writes event N with <code>version = N</code>; one rule for both: apply only if my version ≥ the row's. Batch landing after the stream applied X+1 is skipped; the reverse order applies X+1 on top. Last-writer-wins with a log position as the clock, which is the safe kind.</p><p><b>Option B, build new, replay, flip.</b> Write the inbox as a new table from the snapshot at X, run a consumer on the new table from offset X until lag is near zero, flip readers. Needs log retention for the rebuild's duration and an idempotent consumer.</p><p>B for schema changes and reconciliation; A as the permanent write rule so batch and stream writes commute. The diff compares only rows with version ≤ X, so post-cutoff events never show as false mismatches.</p>",
        line: "anchor the batch to a log offset; version rows by offset and apply-if-newer, or build new, replay from the offset, then flip." },
      { q: "The search index full rebuild: input, join, partitioning, and replacing it without downtime",
        robby: "Tokenizing right (terms only, intersect at query time). The join was missing: messages are keyed by conversation and don't carry the reader. And pivoted from full rebuild to \"just add things after the last ID.\"",
        a: "<p><b>Tokenize:</b> one message in, one <code>((user_id, term), msg_id, position)</code> per word out; position enables phrase search. <b>The join:</b> \"all messages a user sent or received\" means fanning each message out to every participant, a join of messages with participants on conv_id before tokenizing, and it's where the hot key lives: a 200,000-member group turns each message into 200,000 postings per term. <b>Partition</b> by <code>hash(user_id)</code>, the inbox's hash, so a user's search and inbox share a shard; sort by (user_id, term); each reducer writes an immutable sorted index file: an LSM segment. <b>Replace:</b> new segment set to a new directory, serving nodes copy, manifest pointer flips, old set kept.</p><p>The incremental path isn't free: appending means new small segments, queries consult every segment, periodic merges, deletes as tombstones until merged. The nightly rebuild bounds the segment count and reconciles the incremental path. Both, not either.</p>",
        line: "explode to (user, term) after joining participants, shard by the inbox's hash, write segments, flip a manifest; incremental on top, rebuild underneath." },
      { q: "What does \"indexed once per conversation\" mean for the big groups? And how does anyone know which mode a conversation is in?",
        robby: "\"Would we put an array of user IDs in the conversations table?\" Then: \"Is there application logic that says if member count < 500 use this key?\"",
        a: "<p>Two indexes with different keys. Small conversations: <code>(user_id, term)</code>, fanned out per participant, one local lookup per search. Big conversations: <code>(conv_id, term)</code>, indexed once, no fan-out. Query: the user's own postings, plus, for each big conversation they belong to (from the participants table, already there), that conversation's postings; merge. Chapter 7's local-vs-global secondary index, chosen per conversation.</p><p><b>The decision is a stored flag on the conversation row</b> (<code>index_mode</code>), not a live count. A job sets it with hysteresis (per-conversation above 600, back below 400). The indexer and the query path both read the flag, so they can't disagree; a flipped flag means read both indexes until the next rebuild has moved the history. Same shape as the owner epoch: a durable decision everyone reads instead of a judgment each component makes alone.</p>",
        line: "two keys, one flag on the conversation row with hysteresis, and every reader reads the same flag." },
      { q: "Backfill edited_at for two years of history. What must it never do to the live Cassandra cluster?",
        robby: "\"Write to an offline cluster and swap over.\" Too big a hammer for one column. And \"if it's columnar, write a new column file\" is the lakehouse answer; Cassandra has no column file to add.",
        a: "<p><b>Value:</b> edits are events with <code>ref_seq</code>, so edited_at is the max edit timestamp per (conv_id, ref_seq). <b>Delivery:</b> input from a snapshot or the lake copy, never a live scan; the reducer emits Cassandra's native on-disk files partitioned by the cluster's token ranges (partitioned hash join in the wild); bulk-load with the streaming loader, bypassing commit log and memtable, throttled per node, one rack at a time, off-peak; then wait for compaction.</p><p><b>Never:</b> write through the client path; scan the live cluster for input; <b>overwrite a newer live value</b> (the backfilled cell carries the edit event's timestamp as its write timestamp, so per-cell last-writer-wins keeps today's real edit); load faster than compaction absorbs. And ask first whether you need the column: it's derivable on read.</p>",
        line: "emit the store's own files from a snapshot, bulk-load them throttled, version the cell by the event's time; the best backfill is the one you skip." },
      { q: "Analytics: DAU, messages per day, p99 group size. Source, format, and why not the online stores?",
        robby: "\"DAU from a last_activity column on users, messages per day by secondary-keying the messages table on timestamp.\" A last-activity column is one overwritten timestamp: today's DAU and nothing else. And p99 was skipped.",
        a: "<p>The source is an <b>event log</b>: message_sent, message_delivered, conversation_opened, with user, conversation, timestamp, region, landed in object storage as columnar files partitioned by date. DAU is yesterday's directory, count distinct user; weekly actives is seven directories; messages per day is a row count. p99 group size is a state question: participants snapshot, count per conv_id, 99th percentile.</p><p><b>Why not online:</b> load; smeared reads with no cutoff; wrong shape (Cassandra is keyed by conversation, so \"yesterday\" is a full-cluster scan, and a timestamp secondary index on the write-heaviest table is the thing chapter 7 said never to build); wrong engine (no distinct count or percentile across a cluster); no history. Output: a tiny aggregate table, one row per day per metric, which is what the dashboard reads.</p>",
        line: "events to date-partitioned columnar files, state from snapshots, aggregates to a small table; the online stores have the wrong shape and no history." },
      { q: "A 200,000-member group is the hot key in every per-conversation job. Show the skew and fix it.",
        robby: "Gave the structural fix (per-conversation storage behind a flag) and no numbers.",
        a: "<p>Inbox fan-out join, yesterday: the median group has 4 participants and 20 messages, so its reducer emits 80 rows; the big group has 200,000 participants and 1,000 messages, so its reducer emits <b>200,000,000</b> rows while its neighbors idle. Job time equals that reducer's time.</p><p><b>Fixes in order:</b> don't fan out the big group at all (flag it to per-conversation storage; removes the hot key instead of handling it); handle it inside the jobs that must touch it (salt plus replicated small side, or map-side join for the flagged conversations); cap group size as a product decision, because the biggest group sets the worst case of every per-conversation job. The trap: the online path and the batch job must read the same flag or data goes missing silently.</p>",
        line: "the biggest group is the worst case of every per-conversation job; cap it, and move anything above a few hundred to pull-based storage." },
      { q: "Join messages (Cassandra) with users (Postgres) for an abuse model. Which join, and how do both inputs get to the same place?",
        robby: "\"Scan messages for bad actors first, then pass the short list to Postgres for user IDs.\" A real optimization (push the filter below the join) aimed at inference, not training; and it scans Cassandra live and point-looks-up production Postgres.",
        a: "<p>Training needs features for <i>every</i> sender, abusers and normal users alike, or there's nothing to learn the difference from: account age, messages per day, reply rate, report count, contact overlap. No filter to push.</p><p><b>Get both out:</b> messages via Cassandra's nightly snapshot to date-partitioned columnar files; users via a nightly dump or, better, Postgres's change log into the lake. Both anchored to a cutoff. <b>Join:</b> users is a few gigabytes, so broadcast hash on sender_id with a size check; sort-merge if it outgrows memory; partitioned hash is impossible by construction (two systems, two hashes). <b>Output:</b> a per-user feature table and a model file; the model goes to the send path by file swap, and the filter-first pass joins against the feature table at inference time, where it belongs.</p>",
        line: "land both in the lake first; broadcast the small side with a guard; the filter-first trick is for inference, not training." },
      { q: "The job ran over the 2:00 am snapshot and 2:05 am messages are missing. Bug? What do you tell the PM?",
        a: "<p>By design: the cutoff is the feature, and the output is exactly right as of 2:00 am. <b>The bug would be the opposite:</b> some 2:05 messages in and some out is a smeared cutoff. <b>Give the PM a contract:</b> \"data as of 2:00 am, published by 6:00 am, daily,\" with the as-of timestamp on the dashboard; anything fresher than a day is stream processing with the batch as the nightly reconcile.</p><p>The real edge is fuzzier than 2:00: a Cassandra snapshot isn't one instant across nodes, so a 1:59:58 write to two of three replicas may or may not be in it. Process only up to a watermark with a safety margin, or anchor the cutoff to a log position.</p>",
        line: "a clean cutoff is the feature; publish the as-of time as a contract, and anchor it to a log position so it's exact." },
      { q: "A deleted-user job must remove everything within 30 days. Batch or online, and how do you prove it ran?",
        robby: "\"Online, because recomputing the entire database for one user isn't scalable.\" False choice; the batch processes the pending <i>list</i>, not one user. Crypto-shredding was the strongest part of the answer.",
        a: "<p>Finding a user's data is not a point lookup: their messages are under (conv_id, seq) across every conversation; copies sit in other users' inbox copies; postings live under every recipient's (user_id, term); the lake holds them in immutable date files and backups keep them 35 days. So: the request is recorded and the account flagged <b>online</b> (reads hide the user within a minute); a <b>daily batch</b> runs over the whole pending list, one pass per snapshot finding every row for every pending user, emitting bulk tombstones, dropping postings at the next rebuild, rewriting affected lake files. A hundred requests cost the same scan as one.</p><p><b>Crypto-shredding</b> for the copies you can't rewrite: per-user content key; delete the key and every copy (backups, lake files, the ones you missed) is unreadable. It's what makes 35-day backups compatible with a 30-day promise.</p><p><b>Proof:</b> an audit row per request (received, flagged, each store's completion time and tombstone count, key destroyed); a separate <b>verification job</b> that scans the next snapshot and asserts zero rows for every user deleted more than a day ago, producing a signed daily report. Keep the report, not the data.</p>",
        line: "flag online, remove by daily batch over the pending list, shred the key for what you can't rewrite, prove it with a separate verifier." },
      { q: "The inbox rebuild writes garbage for 5% of users. Recovery, and what property makes it possible?",
        a: "<p>Throw away the output, fix, re-run, verify, publish; or point back at yesterday's version. <b>Two properties:</b> immutable input lets you re-run, and the job wrote a new version instead of overwriting, so yesterday still exists. The inbox is also stream-fed, so rollback has a second step: re-point to yesterday's output and replay the stream from yesterday's offset, or the 95% lose today's messages. And move verification <i>before</i> the flip: row count within a few percent of yesterday, a sampled diff against the live copy, schema checks; a failed gate leaves the pointer alone and pages on-call instead of shipping garbage.</p>",
        line: "re-run or re-point, then replay the stream from the old offset; and gate the flip so the garbage never goes live." },
      { q: "The 9-hour Spark job for the search index, 6-hour window: three levers in order",
        robby: "\"Not familiar enough with Spark.\" Nothing here is Spark-specific.",
        a: "<p><b>Step zero: look at the stage timings.</b> One task running for hours while the rest finish in minutes is skew; all tasks uniformly slow is volume. Which picture decides the first lever.</p><ol><li><b>Kill the skew:</b> the 200,000-member group in the participants join; flag it, salt it, or map-side join it. A nine-hour job where eight hours were one reducer becomes ninety minutes.</li><li><b>Shrink the shuffle:</b> tokenize and project before the shuffle so (user_id, term, msg_id, pos) crosses, not the message row; drop deleted and media-only messages before the join; broadcast participants if it fits; read columnar, date-partitioned input.</li><li><b>Do less per night:</b> index only what changed into new segments and rebuild weekly, or roll the rebuild one user-shard per night.</li><li><b>Last, more machines:</b> one config line, only works for the uniform picture, costs money every night, and hides what levers 1 and 2 would have removed.</li></ol>",
        line: "measure, skew, shuffle, incremental, hardware; say \"add machines\" first and the follow-up is \"what if it's skew.\"" }
    ]},
    { id: "followups", title: "Interviewer follow-ups", items: [
      { q: "\"Why not just run the analytics query against the production database at 3 am?\"",
        a: "<p>No guarantee it finishes before load returns, so you kill it and got nothing for the cost; 3 am is somebody's peak if you're international; the result is wrong-shaped anyway (no cutoff, and the online store can't do a distinct count or percentile across a cluster); it couples analytics to the production schema so a column rename breaks a dashboard; and it works for a year until the table grows and the job creeps into the European peak.</p>",
        line: "it's load you can't bound, a result you can't trust, a coupling you'll regret, and it works until it doesn't." },
      { q: "\"What's the difference between a data warehouse and a data lake, and which here?\"",
        robby: "\"A warehouse is like Postgres sharded; a lake is Parquet; I don't know why I'd pick one.\" A warehouse is a managed columnar system that owns its storage, not a row store sharded.",
        a: "<p>Warehouse: a managed columnar analytics system that owns its storage, enforces schema at load, handles compaction, indexing, and access control, SQL only (Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift). Lake: files you own in object storage, schema at read, any engine. Lakehouse: a lake with a table format giving it the warehouse's transactions and schema.</p><p><b>Here:</b> the data feeds things that aren't SQL (the search index build, model training, the deletion and reconcile jobs), so the lake is the system of record for the event log and the snapshots, one copy read by every engine, with a warehouse-style SQL layer on top for analysts reading the same files. \"Everyone's going that way\" is the reason to give last.</p>",
        line: "the warehouse sells a managed governed SQL box; the lake sells open files any tool reads; here, lake as source, SQL as one consumer." },
      { q: "\"Your nightly job failed halfway. What state is the system in?\"",
        robby: "Answered \"what do you do\" (hardware vs code vs took production down). The question asks what state it's in, and the interviewer is checking whether you designed the job so the answer is boring.",
        a: "<p><b>If designed right: exactly the state it was in before the job started.</b> Input untouched; half-finished files are scratch nobody reads; the pointer never moved so yesterday's version is still served; no side effects escaped. Recovery is fix or wait, then re-run, resuming from the last checkpoint. The only cost is a day more staleness, and missing the window should page.</p><p><b>If not:</b> you don't know, and that's the point: an unknown fraction of rows updated in place, an unknown subset of notifications sent and about to be re-sent, maybe production down with it.</p>",
        line: "same state as before it started, because output goes to a new location and the pointer flips only on success." },
      { q: "\"MapReduce is dead. Why are we still talking about it?\"",
        a: "<p>Spark and Flink reimplement its model rather than run on it. The primitives are unchanged (partition by key, shuffle, sort-merge and hash joins, skew handling); the cost model is unchanged (\"where's the shuffle\" is still how you reason about any batch job); the fault-tolerance contract is unchanged (immutable input, no side effects, output only on success); and it's the vocabulary: \"what does your mapper emit\" is a Spark question in MapReduce words. Dead the way assembly is dead.</p>",
        line: "nobody writes it and everything compiles down to its ideas." },
      { q: "\"How do you know the index the batch job built is correct before you swap it in?\"",
        robby: "\"If yesterday worked and you didn't change the job, assume today works.\" The code didn't change; the data did. Unchanged code on changed data is the most common way a nightly job goes wrong.",
        a: "<p>Gates on the <i>output</i>, run inside the job before the pointer moves: (1) every task completed; (2) <b>volume against yesterday</b>: total postings, rows per shard, file sizes within a few percent, no shard at zero (catches the empty-snapshot and dropped-column class, cheapest gate, catches the most); (3) format: segments parse, keys sorted, no duplicates; (4) distribution: postings per user, distinct terms, top hundred terms close to yesterday's shape (catches a tokenizer regression); (5) <b>sampled correctness</b>: a thousand random messages found under every participant, a thousand deleted ones absent, plus golden queries including a big-group member; (6) diff against the live incremental index for rows older than the cutoff; (7) canary then phased rollout by user shard, watching zero-result rate, latency, errors; (8) old version retained a full day.</p>",
        line: "the job doesn't publish until it has proven its output: volume, format, distribution, samples, diff, canary." },
      { q: "\"Explain a shuffle to a junior engineer in three sentences.\"",
        robby: "\"The shuffle sorts your data by key so it's colocated, which makes it easier to count.\" Missing the word that costs money: partition.",
        a: "<p>After the mappers emit key-value pairs, the shuffle hashes each key to pick which reducer owns it and sends every record over the network to that machine. Each reducer then sorts what it received, so all values for one key sit next to each other. That's why the reducer can finish each key in a single pass, and why the shuffle is where a job's network cost and its skew both come from.</p>",
        line: "hash to pick the owner, move it, sort it; one pass per key, and that's where the cost and the skew live." },
      { q: "\"The job is deterministic, but two runs produced different output. Name three causes.\"",
        robby: "Timestamps, and the input picked up more data. Two of the list, and the second is the big one.",
        a: "<p>Every cause is a hidden input. The input wasn't actually fixed (snapshot cutoff differed, read a live table, an upstream file replaced). The clock. Order the code didn't control: hash-map iteration, \"first value wins\" over unsorted values, floating-point sums in partition order. An external lookup fetched at run time: a dictionary, a model, a config, a flag. The environment: a library upgrade, a tokenizer version, locale or timezone. Random without a seed. And the non-cause to rule out first: output files written in task-completion order; sort before you diff.</p>",
        line: "a job is deterministic only over all its inputs, and the hidden ones are the clock, iteration order, and anything fetched at run time." },
      { q: "\"How would you make this batch pipeline incremental instead of a full rebuild every night?\"",
        a: "<p>Consume the change log instead of a snapshot; the job never ends and reads from an offset. Each event becomes a small write: new index segments merged periodically with deletes as tombstones (the LSM engine), or one inbox row update. Every write carries the event's offset as its version with apply-if-newer. Checkpoint the offset with the state so a crash resumes and replays harmlessly. The cutoff becomes a watermark.</p><p><b>What stays batch:</b> the reconcile (the incremental state drifts), schema changes (a backfill), compaction and tombstone cleanup, anything needing a consistent cutoff like the deletion proof. <b>Lambda:</b> batch for correctness and stream for freshness, merged at query time; robust, twice the code. <b>Kappa:</b> stream only, rebuilds by replaying the log through the same consumer; one code path, demands full retention and fast replay. Either way: online serves it, the stream keeps it fresh, the batch rebuilds it and checks the stream's work.</p>",
        line: "consume the log, version by offset, checkpoint; keep the batch for reconcile, schema changes, and compaction." }
    ]}
  ],
  takeaways: [
    "A batch job is a pure function over immutable input to derived output in a new location. Everything else, re-runs, rollback, automatic retry, safe experimentation, falls out of that one rule, and it holds only if the job has no side effects.",
    "Mappers are assigned by input block and reducers by key. A mapper's work is capped by the block; a reducer's by nothing. Skew is born at the shuffle, and no hash function can split one key.",
    "The shuffle is partition then sort: hash the key to pick the owner, move it over the network, sort so one key's values are adjacent. It is where the network cost and the skew both live, and a combiner is how you shrink it for an aggregation.",
    "Three joins by one question: does a side fit in memory? Both big, sort-merge (everything crosses). One fits, broadcast (ship it, no shuffle, guard the size). Both already partitioned the same way, partitioned hash (no shuffle, but someone already paid for it).",
    "Hot keys: find them first (sample or declare), then salt from the record and replicate the other side, or map-side join them, or pre-aggregate, or cap them as a product decision. The biggest group sets the worst case of every per-conversation job.",
    "Dataflow engines kept MapReduce's primitives and contract and dropped the forced disk writes, sorts, and waiting. The price is recompute-on-failure, which demands deterministic operators and earns checkpoints at expensive stages.",
    "Never write batch output row by row into a live store. Build the store's own files, copy them, flip a pointer, keep the old set. It is the only way the output stays all-or-nothing and the only way rollback is one pointer.",
    "Batch goes stale; a patched view goes incorrect. Online serves it, the stream keeps it fresh, the batch rebuilds it from the source and diffs to find how far the stream drifted. Both, not either.",
    "Anchor every cutoff to a log position, not a clock, and version derived rows by that position with apply-if-newer. That is what lets a batch backfill and a live stream write the same rows without clobbering each other.",
    "Know which store you are talking to. Cassandra, a Parquet lake, and a warehouse each have a shape; the right answer for one (write a column file, bulk-load SSTables, swap a cluster) is wrong for the others, and the chapter is mostly about putting the data where the job can use it."
  ]
});
